        <Data name="county"><value>Tuscola</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <styleUrl>#nrhp_site</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-83.17436759399999,43.60219028500017,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Malcolm X House</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://services3.arcgis.com/Jdnp1TjADvSDxMAX/arcgis/rest/services/Historical_Markers_Public_View/FeatureServer/0/1868/attachments/2415" width="320"/><br/><p>Malcolm X Shabazz
Malcolm Little (1925-1965) arrived at this house on August 8, 1952. He  joined the Nation of Islam (NOI) soon after, taking the name Malcolm X.      He became the assistant minister at Detroit’s Temple No. 1 in mid-1953. He was later sent to the East Coast to aid in recruiting and founding temples. In 1958 he proposed to Betty Dean Sanders from a Detroit phone booth. They wed a few days later in Lansing. In December 1963 Malcolm gave his “Message to the Grass Roots” speech at King Solomon Baptist Church in Detroit. He left NOI in March 1964   and started two new organization…</p><p>County: Wayne &middot; City: Inkster</p><p>Date reported: 2022</p>]]></description>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="category"><value>Historic places (NRHP)</value></Data>
        <Data name="date_reported"><value>2022</value></Data>
